  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nLow pressure is set to quickly track through the St. Lawrence \nValley this afternoon. This will spread rain across the forecast\narea early this morning.\n\nThe best overlap of forcing and moisture is on the front-end,\nand guidance has most QPF falling between 8am and 2pm. The quick\nmoving low will remain flat and progressive, keeping a slot of\ndry air in between the warm front in the morning, and the cold\nfront mid to late afternoon. Thus it is likely the more steady\nrain is focused through the morning, with showery \ncharacteristics for the afternoon. Flooding is not anticipated\nbased on forecast amounts, but could see some instances of\nnuisance/poor drainage flooding where leaves may hinder drain \nperformance.\n\nAs the warm front lifts through the area, could see some fog\ndevelop amid scattered showers through mid afternoon. The final\nact of the day will be the cold front's arrival, potentially\nwith some low-topped convection into SW NH. MUCAPE values,\naround 200 j/kg are much less than evening lightning on\nTuesday. Thunder may be limited here, but the vertical mixing\nwill likely promote a rapid wind shift with sudden gusts as the\nfront pushes east.\n\nThe front's passage will mark the beginning of NW winds\novernight and into Saturday, ushering in cooler temperatures and\nwind chill.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAs the aforementioned low pressure system departs northeast,\nbroad high pressure builds into the Great Lakes and Ohio Valley.\nThe pressure gradient between these two systems will tighten to\nproduce gusty NW winds Friday night through the day Saturday.\nThere has been some slackening of the forecast gradient the last\nfew forecasts, and this has resulted in wind forecasts not as\nstrong as early week. However, the wind combined with the cooler\nairmass will certainly leave an impact on the area.\n\nDaytime highs will struggle to make it out of the 40s along the\nimmediate coast Saturday, with widespread 30s and 40s across the\nmountains and foothills/interior. With frequent gusts 25 to 35\nmph, resultant wind chill values may reside in the 30s across\nmost of the area.\n\nThe effect is more pronounced for the higher terrain, where \nwinter conditions, including snow, are expected with greater \nwind and wind chill magnitude. Wind gusts over 100 mph are \nexpected on Mount Washington on Saturday. Any hikers or those \nabove 2500ft should plan for wintertime conditions this weekend.\n\nThe coldest night of the season arrives Saturday night with\nwidespread lows in the 20s. Lows below freezing are expected to\nthe coastline across the region.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\n\nHigh pressure crests over the region on Sunday and looks to \nstay nosed in through at least the daylight hours of next \nThursday. This will bring an extended period of mild daytime \ntemperatures, and likely some pretty chilly nights due to \nradiational cooling, as well as dry weather.\n\nSunday will be the transition day with high temperatures in the\nlow to mid-50s with mostly clear skies, but after a raw \nSaturday this will probably feel comfortable. Afterwards, most \ndays should feature abundant sunshine with high temperatures in \nthe upper 50s and 60s. Periods of clouds are possible with \nsubtle troughing aloft, but the midlevel airmass looks very dry \nwhich will preclude any threat for precipitation. Low \ntemperatures look to be generally around normal being in the \nupper 20s to mid-30s north to south, but of course any night \nwhere strong radiational cooling takes place, can end up quite \ncold. Looking at long range ensembles 850-700 mb temperatures \nstay right around the 0 C mark through this stretch so certainly\nsome cold nights could come to fruition, but nothing crazy.",
